#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097522} :activity/id #uuid "b8439109-03d0-56d9-8a44-f5a5b744e4b7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048742 :topic/id #uuid "ba72c50b-60dc-50dc-ae9b-3cc018d38d60" :topic/math-academy-id 1049} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097522 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097843 :tutorial/content "Consider a system of linear equations and its respective augmented matrix $M\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/7c/7c2283c7de572acfc58b443499598554ecf3d26b1a13c48c80b8d6aadf7b576a.svg)\n\nThe matrix $M$ is already in row echelon form, but we can transform it even further into the **reduced row echelon form** (RREF) using the standard **Gauss-Jordan** method. \n\nThe algorithm is similar to the algorithm of converting a matrix to row echelon form, except that we need to make *all* the pivot entries equal to $1$ and *all* the entries below *and above* a pivot (in a pivot column) equal to $0.$\n\nIn our case, we convert $M$ to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final matrix is in reduced row echelon form:\n\n- all the pivot entries are equal to $1$ (here we have $\\color{red}1$'s on the main diagonal), and\n\n- all the entries below and above a pivot are equal to ${\\color{blue}0}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ce056e74-9707-52d3-b172-483ca14e1f8f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3160 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "78885948-fa6f-5f46-8f96-e5da861960dd" :step/math-academy-id 17209 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097523}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097523 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097844 :tutorial/content "For a $3\\times 3$ system with $3$ pivots, the Gauss-Jordan method can be described as follows:\n\n1\\. First, reduce the matrix to row echelon form using the standard Gaussian elimination method (also making all pivot entries equal to $1$). \n  ![](images/5c/5c0f5ac3d95843b142c90e8ce83378ea2b2ad693baba6670e0cde46a449f5225.svg)\n\n2\\. We use the third row and necessary row operations to make the entries above $a_{33}$ equal to $0.$ Namely, \n\n  - if $a_{23} \\neq 0$, then we use the row operation $R_2:=R_2 + (-a_{23})R_3$, and\n\n  - if $a_{13} \\neq 0$, then we use the row operation $R_1:=R_1 + (-a_{13})R_3$.\n\n3\\. We use the second row and necessary row operations to make the entries above $a_{22}$ equal to $0$. Namely, \n\n  - if $a_{12} \\neq 0$, then we use the row operation $R_1:=R_1 + (-a_{12})R_2$.\n\nCarrying out the above algorithm will convert the matrix to reduced row echelon form.\n\n![](images/9c/9c817628a3c646a928f12548a109b8136bb0a5977fab6e4d18e972690d81159b.svg)\n\n**Note:** Unlike the row echelon form, the *reduced* row echelon form of a matrix is unique." :tutorial/id #uuid "f74e6075-9c2a-5495-ab21-470711559f2a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3161 :tutorial/title "General Algorithm (RREF)"} :step/id #uuid "278dc07d-f5da-5098-a755-d5a72662e2ac" :step/math-academy-id 17210 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097524}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097524 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096674 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096461 :question/id #uuid "de738faf-e020-5058-b6a5-48020212fbc4" :question/math-academy-id "e-4709"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7294399b-b139-5ee6-824c-5e28e4fc49ff" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049058}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546233} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097184} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097185} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288001} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288002} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288004} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288005} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388237} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416435} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567020} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567071} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567075}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Reduced Row Echelon Form of a 3x3 System That Is Already in Row Echelon Form"} :step/id #uuid "7f602ae0-3af8-588f-bdb5-c0995e19908f" :step/math-academy-id 17211 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097525}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097525 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096675 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096462 :question/id #uuid "239a008b-4c67-5334-8285-3c46b773fead" :question/math-academy-id "e-4710"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "95a32907-8304-5dfd-8759-9e91503445bf" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531730} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097173} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241159} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241160} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266101} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266102} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266103} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266104} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364358} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386209} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393410} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567044} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567085}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Reduced Row Echelon Form of a 3x3 System That Is Not in Row Echelon Form"} :step/id #uuid "c80be1fb-c96f-5733-8d79-5c777f840ef9" :step/math-academy-id 17212 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097636}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097634 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096745 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096546 :question/id #uuid "283e9a4a-443f-579f-b272-248446d3a49f" :question/math-academy-id "e-6233"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b010a518-7aa3-5b53-9a6d-ef0779ff0dfa" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050178}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557575} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097353} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097354} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266105} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266106} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344951} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344952} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344953} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363094} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363101} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403773} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403780} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416436} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416437} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422479} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567107}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Reduced Row Echelon Form of a Singular 3x3 System With Two Pivots"} :step/id #uuid "f3553e51-447b-5d4a-b387-1bc43d2a825d" :step/math-academy-id 23346 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097637}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097635 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096746 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096547 :question/id #uuid "8de46974-719c-5ac2-a43c-83d1c67c57b6" :question/math-academy-id "e-6234"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa91091b-5368-5d80-b87d-08b3524177f3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097355} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097356} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266107} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344945} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344948} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344949} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388244} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393424} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400618} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416438} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422478} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567091}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Reduced Row Echelon Form of a Singular 3x3 System With One Pivot"} :step/id #uuid "4208bcd7-ee35-5977-8afd-7beb404e8b75" :step/math-academy-id 23347} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097636 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097869 :tutorial/content "When a $3\\times3$ system of equations has two pivot columns instead of three, we can still reduce its augmented matrix to reduced row echelon form. The process is similar to what we did before. \n\n1\\. First, reduce the matrix to row echelon form using the standard Gaussian elimination method (also making all pivot entries equal to $1$). \n   ![](images/99/992cfaabdb3490e99553fbbdfee0089b399306e3e65720fc6a70014bfa35321a.svg)\n\n2\\. We use the second row and necessary row operations to make the entries above $a_{22}$ equal to $0$. Namely, \n\n  - if $a_{12} \\neq 0$, then we use the row operation $R_1:=R_1 + (-a_{12})R_2$.\n\nCarrying out the above algorithm will convert the matrix to reduced row echelon form.\n\n![](images/3d/3da5751a339ff2ee85d424b42a72f5270f1be076407a33abd1b79e0f082639d5.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "baed23fb-560e-56ad-aea3-46f3728ebfff" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3949 :tutorial/title "Using the Gauss-Jordan Method on a 3x3 System With Two Pivots"} :step/id #uuid "e4f6891c-3e25-562a-900b-9617b1000f05" :step/math-academy-id 23416 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097634}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097637 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097870 :tutorial/content "It's also possible to write the augmented matrix for a $3\\times 3$ system of linear equations with only one pivot in reduced row echelon form. \n\nLet's see an example of how this works." :tutorial/id #uuid "e3748ee3-9d5d-5a6a-808f-ef8f99cbd1aa" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3950 :tutorial/title "Using the Gauss-Jordan Method on a 3x3 System With One Pivot"} :step/id #uuid "541dad41-3dc8-5018-acca-4d79ebbbda41" :step/math-academy-id 23417 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097635}}] :activity/title "Reduced Row Echelon Form" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096857}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422481} :activity/id #uuid "fda0e0b8-3081-5e52-8593-c6700e3052bb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048742 :topic/id #uuid "ba72c50b-60dc-50dc-ae9b-3cc018d38d60" :topic/math-academy-id 1049} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422480 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422479 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ee5bd89-c4e9-5aea-ba6a-3f045af5c0e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 11 & -14 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422467} {:answer/id #uuid "75130a5b-9607-5217-b80c-6757c94daf70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 11 & -14 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422471} {:answer/id #uuid "8076b85b-1614-5135-bb4c-2ab5d41377da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -7 & -14 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422472} {:answer/id #uuid "d64479b7-f216-5594-be33-53f9d09c2c72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -7 & -14 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422473} {:answer/id #uuid "f1ebe9c7-57ca-5f13-b184-b18fa5a512d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & -14 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422475}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422472} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4a2cf38-1593-52f1-9b63-551383c8a50f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422477}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "df6c8a6d-153a-5484-848d-1043ee95c582" :question/math-academy-id "q-74621" :question/problem "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x-11y+5z &=-1 \\\\ x-3y+2z &=1 \\\\ -3x+9y-6z &=-3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -11 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 9 & -6 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -11 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 9 & -6 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -11 & 5 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 9 & -6 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 9 & -6 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the row echelon form above, we have only two pivots (in the $1$st and $2$nd columns). To transform the matrix to reduced row echelon form, we need to create zeros above the pivots only. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -7 & -14 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "06ed1b32-abf8-5d02-b409-e83aa1a9bf69" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422482}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422481 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097174 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f53140c-1654-5c33-b318-d2cc5cdbf3e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨3,2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416405} {:answer/id #uuid "3b8ed053-8c5f-5809-b29d-46bc30062dd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨3,-2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416409} {:answer/id #uuid "52ed3b97-c09d-5bac-bedf-d8cb30fd9295" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨2,-3,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416411} {:answer/id #uuid "ad204329-d0a2-52cb-bab5-d155cba3473f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-3,-2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416423} {:answer/id #uuid "fa5e31d8-6742-527d-a027-3e41d829bbd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-3,-2,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416428}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416409} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d9c6d72-c8a0-5fe2-aea3-51adff0f94d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416431}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cb5d2e37-01c6-52dc-86a3-0e3d22b79684" :question/math-academy-id "q-20267" :question/problem "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y+z &=2 \\\\ x+2y+z &=0 \\\\ x+y+2z &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}-{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=3 \\\\ y &=-2 \\\\ z &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we have the solution $x=3$, $y=-2$, and $z=1.$ Or, in vector form, $⟨3,-2,1⟩.$"} :step/id #uuid "1f437206-fa08-5382-b319-4b882827b9a2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422483}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422482 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266102 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "083caf03-1501-5cba-8190-446a66b010a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-2,3,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263075} {:answer/id #uuid "9af8bfe2-063c-5dba-b504-5816b98f9766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨2,-3,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264325} {:answer/id #uuid "b8b913e9-5eb2-5d55-9bc3-2f3b88be543b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨2,-3,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264598} {:answer/id #uuid "da1a8356-2e88-513c-b7fa-151c2d4478c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨3,-2,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264902} {:answer/id #uuid "f5e3c0bf-feab-56d8-8788-d7833ba1031b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-2,3,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265152}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264325} :answer-field/id #uuid "71bd9945-c16d-52b1-9144-9035edc43bba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265440}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8ff5175c-29cf-5e1c-8b8a-50a809738a87" :question/math-academy-id "q-131618" :question/problem "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y+2z &=5 \\\\ 2x-y+7z &=7 \\\\ x-y+3z &=5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 7 & 7 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 7 & 7 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}-2{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2 \\\\ y &=-3 \\\\ z &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we have the solution $x=2$, $y=-3$, and $z=0.$ Or, in vector form, $⟨2,-3,0⟩.$"} :step/id #uuid "6862e7b5-f79b-5b64-a612-6d85862752ef" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422484}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422483 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342242 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47f1079e-5752-5de3-a9e1-e19c06fada97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339766} {:answer/id #uuid "599056ef-e9ef-5488-965a-9dd9e4140202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339942} {:answer/id #uuid "a3e2972b-b0c5-5496-8789-531b77f4e97c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340614} {:answer/id #uuid "bac13010-faae-5ec6-a632-601054299d76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340802} {:answer/id #uuid "f4632778-f58f-50b4-b85c-9458c6830a8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341280}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340614} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5965582-0f9c-5d43-87a1-fef40d8e1c11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341788}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a1acb1c2-35d8-56f8-bcad-3b6d0c4f6e46" :question/math-academy-id "q-74601" :question/problem "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y-z &=3 \\\\ -2x-3y+2z &=0 \\\\ 2x+3y-z &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6e7d6a76-cc67-5b59-8e1b-d53698eacff0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422480}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422484 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422478 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a7564c8-04d6-57a1-9a06-28d7b8421a35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}y+2z+2 \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422466} {:answer/id #uuid "3c1f7437-d98a-5bd7-97a0-2e5ff4f2f649" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422468} {:answer/id #uuid "41cef48b-98a3-54bd-b406-d5e167d64ae9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2-y+2z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422469} {:answer/id #uuid "45df3fe7-8c4e-5624-afa2-3e1ab32743c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}y-2z-2 \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422470} {:answer/id #uuid "d82b73e6-0a25-5302-99e8-b157a818667e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}y-2z-1 \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422474}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422470} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d6b9331-1da4-5d89-9d4a-e8d26ffe05e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422476}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "86e15f08-648b-5bcc-988f-7551732eb466" :question/math-academy-id "q-131649" :question/problem "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x+2y-4z &=4 \\\\ -x+y-2z &=2 \\\\ x-y+2z &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -4 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -4 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -4 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -4 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y+2z &=-2 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z &=0 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final two equations, we can remove these equations from the system. \n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$, and we have two free variables, $y,$ and $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y$ and $z.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=y-2z-2 \\\\ y &=y \\\\ z &=z,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ and $z$ (the free variables) are arbitrary numbers.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}y-2z-2 \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9f34ebb7-b2e8-595a-840d-d43ea1cd168d"}] :activity/title "Reduced Row Echelon Form" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422465}]}
